1
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:11,640
Tonight,

2
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:13,080
step outside and look up

3
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:15,680
Now imagine that the Moon is simply gone

4
00:00:15,926 --> 00:00:16,676
No explosion

5
00:00:16,676 --> 00:00:17,756
No falling rocks

6
00:00:17,756 --> 00:00:18,756
One second it's there,

7
00:00:18,756 --> 00:00:20,557
and the next second it isn't

8
00:00:20,707 --> 00:00:22,257
Would the oceans flood the coasts?

9
00:00:22,257 --> 00:00:23,737
Would Earth fly out of its orbit?

10
00:00:23,737 --> 00:00:25,419
Would life even notice?

11
00:00:25,569 --> 00:00:27,479
Some changes would hit us that very night

12
00:00:27,479 --> 00:00:29,620
Others would take millions of years

13
00:00:29,770 --> 00:00:30,840
This is what would happen,

14
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:31,800
step by step,

15
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:33,880
if the Moon disappeared

16
00:00:36,850 --> 00:00:39,450
Let's say it happens at exactly midnight

17
00:00:39,765 --> 00:00:41,155
Half the planet is in daylight,

18
00:00:41,155 --> 00:00:43,075
and most people there wouldn't notice anything

19
00:00:43,075 --> 00:00:44,143
at all

20
00:00:44,293 --> 00:00:45,123
On the night side,

21
00:00:45,123 --> 00:00:47,243
millions of people would look up and see an

22
00:00:47,243 --> 00:00:49,843
empty sky where the Moon should be

23
00:00:50,006 --> 00:00:50,676
Within minutes,

24
00:00:50,676 --> 00:00:52,716
the internet would explode with photos,

25
00:00:52,716 --> 00:00:54,476
theories and panic

26
00:00:54,685 --> 00:00:56,035
But here's the first surprise:

27
00:00:56,035 --> 00:00:58,635
Earth wouldn't fly off into space

28
00:00:58,888 --> 00:01:00,878
Earth and the Moon actually circle a shared

29
00:01:00,878 --> 00:01:01,638
balance point,

30
00:01:01,638 --> 00:01:04,238
about 4,700 kilometres from Earth's centre

31
00:01:05,460 --> 00:01:06,210
Without the Moon,

32
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:08,130
that tiny wobble would simply stop,

33
00:01:08,130 --> 00:01:10,370
and Earth would keep circling the Sun almost

34
00:01:10,370 --> 00:01:12,130
exactly as before

35
00:01:12,380 --> 00:01:13,810
So the planet is fine

36
00:01:13,810 --> 00:01:15,770
The real changes start with something much

37
00:01:15,770 --> 00:01:16,250
quieter:

38
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:17,690
the light

39
00:01:20,983 --> 00:01:23,533
A full moon is about 2,000 times brighter than

40
00:01:23,533 --> 00:01:23,933
Venus,

41
00:01:23,933 --> 00:01:26,533
the next brightest thing in the night sky

42
00:01:26,707 --> 00:01:27,217
Without it,

43
00:01:27,217 --> 00:01:29,218
every night would be like the darkest new-moon

44
00:01:29,218 --> 00:01:29,639
night,

45
00:01:29,639 --> 00:01:30,749
forever

46
00:01:30,899 --> 00:01:32,049
Away from city lights,

47
00:01:32,049 --> 00:01:34,329
a full moon makes the ground hundreds of times

48
00:01:34,329 --> 00:01:36,409
brighter than starlight alone

49
00:01:36,658 --> 00:01:38,807
Now there would only be starlight

50
00:01:38,957 --> 00:01:40,667
Astronomers would actually celebrate

51
00:01:40,667 --> 00:01:42,867
The Milky Way would shine across the sky on

52
00:01:42,867 --> 00:01:44,450
every clear night

53
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:45,910
But for millions of animals,

54
00:01:45,910 --> 00:01:47,310
darkness is not just a view

55
00:01:47,310 --> 00:01:49,722
It's a matter of life and death

56
00:01:49,872 --> 00:01:50,822
And the next morning,

57
00:01:50,822 --> 00:01:52,742
people on the coast would notice that the sea

58
00:01:52,742 --> 00:01:54,502
itself had changed

59
00:01:58,017 --> 00:01:59,527
Tides are made mostly by the Moon

60
00:01:59,527 --> 00:02:01,847
Its tidal pull on Earth is about twice as

61
00:02:01,847 --> 00:02:03,603
strong as the Sun's

62
00:02:03,753 --> 00:02:06,183
The Moon stretches the oceans into two bulges,

63
00:02:06,183 --> 00:02:08,619
and Earth spins through them every day

64
00:02:08,769 --> 00:02:09,839
When the Moon vanishes,

65
00:02:09,839 --> 00:02:11,519
those bulges would slowly settle,

66
00:02:11,519 --> 00:02:13,479
and the oceans would slosh as they find a

67
00:02:13,479 --> 00:02:14,877
new balance

68
00:02:15,027 --> 00:02:16,057
No giant tsunami

69
00:02:16,057 --> 00:02:18,419
But the tides would never be the same

70
00:02:18,569 --> 00:02:19,799
Only the Sun would pull now,

71
00:02:19,799 --> 00:02:21,679
so tides would shrink to about a third of

72
00:02:21,679 --> 00:02:22,947
their size

73
00:02:23,097 --> 00:02:24,327
In Canada's Bay of Fundy,

74
00:02:24,327 --> 00:02:26,607
where the water can rise more than 15 metres,

75
00:02:26,607 --> 00:02:28,695
the difference would be dramatic

76
00:02:28,845 --> 00:02:30,635
And there would be no more spring tides or

77
00:02:30,635 --> 00:02:31,355
neap tides

78
00:02:31,355 --> 00:02:33,755
The tide would be roughly the same size every

79
00:02:33,755 --> 00:02:35,325
day of the month

80
00:02:35,475 --> 00:02:35,865
For us,

81
00:02:35,865 --> 00:02:37,185
that's an inconvenience

82
00:02:37,185 --> 00:02:38,945
For the creatures living on the edge of the

83
00:02:38,945 --> 00:02:39,265
sea,

84
00:02:39,265 --> 00:02:41,025
it's a disaster

85
00:02:44,517 --> 00:02:46,747
Between high and low tide lies one of the

86
00:02:46,747 --> 00:02:48,227
busiest places on Earth:

87
00:02:48,227 --> 00:02:49,987
the intertidal zone

88
00:02:50,206 --> 00:02:50,636
Crabs,

89
00:02:50,636 --> 00:02:51,196
mussels,

90
00:02:51,196 --> 00:02:53,436
barnacles and sea stars live here,

91
00:02:53,436 --> 00:02:54,436
covered by water,

92
00:02:54,436 --> 00:02:55,676
then exposed to air,

93
00:02:55,676 --> 00:02:57,162
twice a day

94
00:02:57,312 --> 00:02:59,182
Shorebirds time their meals to the falling

95
00:02:59,182 --> 00:02:59,622
tide,

96
00:02:59,622 --> 00:03:02,222
feeding on the mudflats it leaves behind

97
00:03:02,479 --> 00:03:03,629
With much smaller tides,

98
00:03:03,629 --> 00:03:05,929
this rich strip of coast would shrink

99
00:03:06,079 --> 00:03:06,909
Horseshoe crabs,

100
00:03:06,909 --> 00:03:09,309
which have survived for more than 400 million

101
00:03:09,309 --> 00:03:09,629
years,

102
00:03:09,629 --> 00:03:11,789
crawl ashore to lay eggs on the high tides

103
00:03:11,789 --> 00:03:13,975
around the new and full moon

104
00:03:14,125 --> 00:03:16,155
Small silver fish called grunion ride the

105
00:03:16,155 --> 00:03:18,408
highest waves onto California's beaches to

106
00:03:18,408 --> 00:03:18,715
spawn,

107
00:03:18,715 --> 00:03:21,315
on the nights after the full and new moon

108
00:03:21,626 --> 00:03:23,612
Their whole calendar would be gone

109
00:03:23,762 --> 00:03:24,872
And far out at sea,

110
00:03:24,872 --> 00:03:27,112
the biggest moonlit event on the planet would

111
00:03:27,112 --> 00:03:28,552
fall silent

112
00:03:32,017 --> 00:03:33,687
Every year on the Great Barrier Reef,

113
00:03:33,687 --> 00:03:35,767
something incredible happens a few nights after

114
00:03:35,767 --> 00:03:37,173
a full moon

115
00:03:37,323 --> 00:03:40,153
More than 130 species of coral release their

116
00:03:40,153 --> 00:03:41,033
eggs and sperm,

117
00:03:41,033 --> 00:03:43,385
many of them within the same hour

118
00:03:43,535 --> 00:03:45,605
The water fills with tiny pink and white

119
00:03:45,605 --> 00:03:46,125
bundles,

120
00:03:46,125 --> 00:03:48,205
like an underwater snowstorm

121
00:03:48,586 --> 00:03:50,736
Water temperature tells the corals which month

122
00:03:50,736 --> 00:03:51,276
it is

123
00:03:51,276 --> 00:03:53,557
Moonlight helps tell them which night

124
00:03:53,707 --> 00:03:56,057
Scientists have found that corals can sense the

125
00:03:56,057 --> 00:03:58,224
faint blue light of the Moon

126
00:03:58,374 --> 00:03:59,204
Without that signal,

127
00:03:59,204 --> 00:04:00,404
their timing could drift apart

128
00:04:00,404 --> 00:04:02,404
And if corals release their eggs on different

129
00:04:02,404 --> 00:04:02,804
nights,

130
00:04:02,804 --> 00:04:04,773
far fewer of them meet

131
00:04:04,923 --> 00:04:06,713
Reefs that are already struggling would lose

132
00:04:06,713 --> 00:04:09,313
one of their most powerful ways to recover

133
00:04:09,602 --> 00:04:10,072
On land,

134
00:04:10,072 --> 00:04:12,352
the darkness would quietly change who hunts,

135
00:04:12,352 --> 00:04:14,432
and who gets hunted

136
00:04:17,383 --> 00:04:18,213
In Tanzania,

137
00:04:18,213 --> 00:04:20,613
scientists studied more than a thousand lion

138
00:04:20,613 --> 00:04:23,213
attacks on people over about twenty years

139
00:04:23,595 --> 00:04:25,385
The attacks were most common in the dark

140
00:04:25,385 --> 00:04:27,025
evenings right after the full moon,

141
00:04:27,025 --> 00:04:28,995
when the Moon rises late

142
00:04:29,145 --> 00:04:30,535
Lions hunt best in darkness,

143
00:04:30,535 --> 00:04:32,838
when their prey can't see them coming

144
00:04:32,988 --> 00:04:33,658
Without the Moon,

145
00:04:33,658 --> 00:04:35,586
every night would be a hunting night for ambush

146
00:04:35,586 --> 00:04:36,706
predators

147
00:04:36,913 --> 00:04:37,423
Meanwhile,

148
00:04:37,423 --> 00:04:38,663
on the African savanna,

149
00:04:38,663 --> 00:04:40,743
tiny dung beetles roll their balls in straight

150
00:04:40,743 --> 00:04:42,863
lines by following the polarised light of the

151
00:04:42,863 --> 00:04:43,857
Moon

152
00:04:44,007 --> 00:04:44,797
And in the Arctic,

153
00:04:44,797 --> 00:04:46,437
during the months-long polar night,

154
00:04:46,437 --> 00:04:49,197
tiny sea creatures called zooplankton rise and

155
00:04:49,197 --> 00:04:51,797
sink by following the Moon instead of the Sun

156
00:04:52,332 --> 00:04:53,162
Remove the Moon,

157
00:04:53,162 --> 00:04:55,701
and these ancient rhythms would break

158
00:04:55,851 --> 00:04:57,041
But for some baby animals,

159
00:04:57,041 --> 00:04:58,841
the darkness brings a different danger:

160
00:04:58,841 --> 00:05:00,281
getting lost

161
00:05:03,683 --> 00:05:05,553
When sea turtles hatch on a beach at night,

162
00:05:05,553 --> 00:05:07,911
they don't know where the ocean is

163
00:05:08,061 --> 00:05:09,650
They crawl toward the brightest horizon,

164
00:05:09,650 --> 00:05:11,890
which on a natural beach is usually over the

165
00:05:11,890 --> 00:05:12,250
water,

166
00:05:12,250 --> 00:05:14,650
glowing with moonlight and starlight

167
00:05:14,957 --> 00:05:15,267
Today,

168
00:05:15,267 --> 00:05:17,587
city lights already pull many hatchlings the

169
00:05:17,587 --> 00:05:18,287
wrong way,

170
00:05:18,287 --> 00:05:20,462
onto roads and into danger

171
00:05:20,612 --> 00:05:21,322
Without the Moon,

172
00:05:21,322 --> 00:05:23,162
the glow over the sea would be weaker,

173
00:05:23,162 --> 00:05:25,762
and our lights would win even more often

174
00:05:26,011 --> 00:05:28,201
Moths already get trapped circling our lamps

175
00:05:28,201 --> 00:05:29,241
On moonless nights,

176
00:05:29,241 --> 00:05:30,801
our lights would be the brightest things

177
00:05:30,801 --> 00:05:31,887
around

178
00:05:32,037 --> 00:05:33,107
Even people would change

179
00:05:33,107 --> 00:05:34,787
Our ancestors planned hunts,

180
00:05:34,787 --> 00:05:37,387
journeys and festivals around moonlit nights

181
00:05:37,901 --> 00:05:39,811
And there's one spectacular show that would

182
00:05:39,811 --> 00:05:42,211
disappear from the sky forever

183
00:05:45,550 --> 00:05:47,740
The Sun is about 400 times wider than the

184
00:05:47,740 --> 00:05:48,100
Moon,

185
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:50,700
but also about 400 times farther away

186
00:05:51,402 --> 00:05:53,432
That lucky coincidence is why the Moon can

187
00:05:53,432 --> 00:05:56,032
cover the Sun almost perfectly in a total solar

188
00:05:56,032 --> 00:05:57,152
eclipse

189
00:05:57,405 --> 00:06:00,435
In 1919 an eclipse let scientists photograph

190
00:06:00,435 --> 00:06:01,475
stars near the Sun,

191
00:06:01,475 --> 00:06:04,315
and confirm Einstein's idea that gravity bends

192
00:06:04,315 --> 00:06:05,255
light

193
00:06:05,405 --> 00:06:06,155
Without the Moon,

194
00:06:06,155 --> 00:06:08,195
there would be no total solar eclipses,

195
00:06:08,195 --> 00:06:10,795
and no blood-red lunar eclipses either

196
00:06:11,036 --> 00:06:13,266
Millions of people travel across the world just

197
00:06:13,266 --> 00:06:15,306
to stand in the Moon's shadow for a few

198
00:06:15,306 --> 00:06:16,390
minutes

199
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:18,474
That experience would survive only in old

200
00:06:18,474 --> 00:06:19,908
photos and videos

201
00:06:20,058 --> 00:06:21,608
And the Moon didn't just shape our sky

202
00:06:21,608 --> 00:06:24,208
It shaped the way we count time

203
00:06:27,250 --> 00:06:29,562
The word month comes from the word moon

204
00:06:29,712 --> 00:06:30,782
For thousands of years,

205
00:06:30,782 --> 00:06:33,102
people counted time by watching the Moon grow

206
00:06:33,102 --> 00:06:33,782
and shrink,

207
00:06:33,782 --> 00:06:36,382
about every twenty-nine and a half days

208
00:06:36,736 --> 00:06:39,006
Some ancient stone monuments even seem to be

209
00:06:39,006 --> 00:06:41,606
lined up with the Moon's rising and setting

210
00:06:41,927 --> 00:06:42,357
Today,

211
00:06:42,357 --> 00:06:44,477
more than a billion people still celebrate the

212
00:06:44,477 --> 00:06:45,397
Lunar New Year,

213
00:06:45,397 --> 00:06:47,408
which is set by the Moon

214
00:06:47,558 --> 00:06:49,628
Ramadan begins with the sighting of a new

215
00:06:49,628 --> 00:06:50,108
crescent

216
00:06:50,108 --> 00:06:52,028
Even the date of Easter depends on a full

217
00:06:52,028 --> 00:06:53,039
moon

218
00:06:53,189 --> 00:06:53,939
Without the Moon,

219
00:06:53,939 --> 00:06:56,539
all of these calendars would lose their clock

220
00:06:56,928 --> 00:06:59,238
Fishermen and farmers who still plan their work

221
00:06:59,238 --> 00:07:01,620
by the Moon would have to start over

222
00:07:01,770 --> 00:07:03,760
But the biggest change is hidden in something

223
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:04,680
we never notice:

224
00:07:04,680 --> 00:07:07,080
the length of a day

225
00:07:09,917 --> 00:07:10,347
Every day,

226
00:07:10,347 --> 00:07:12,747
the Moon's tides act like a brake on Earth's

227
00:07:12,747 --> 00:07:13,738
spin

228
00:07:13,888 --> 00:07:14,478
Because of that,

229
00:07:14,478 --> 00:07:15,918
our days are getting longer,

230
00:07:15,918 --> 00:07:17,918
by about two thousandths of a second every

231
00:07:17,918 --> 00:07:19,033
century

232
00:07:19,183 --> 00:07:19,973
At the same time,

233
00:07:19,973 --> 00:07:21,933
the Moon is slowly drifting away,

234
00:07:21,933 --> 00:07:24,333
about 3.8 centimetres every year

235
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,030
We know this because Apollo astronauts left

236
00:07:27,030 --> 00:07:28,070
mirrors on the Moon,

237
00:07:28,070 --> 00:07:30,328
and observatories still bounce lasers off them

238
00:07:30,328 --> 00:07:31,248
today

239
00:07:31,398 --> 00:07:33,948
Fossil shells show that 70 million years ago,

240
00:07:33,948 --> 00:07:35,428
in the age of dinosaurs,

241
00:07:35,428 --> 00:07:38,028
a year had about 372 days

242
00:07:38,945 --> 00:07:41,335
Each day was only about twenty-three and a half

243
00:07:41,335 --> 00:07:42,673
hours long

244
00:07:42,823 --> 00:07:43,573
Without the Moon,

245
00:07:43,573 --> 00:07:45,173
that braking would almost stop

246
00:07:45,173 --> 00:07:47,493
Only the Sun's weaker tides would keep slowing

247
00:07:47,493 --> 00:07:48,699
us down

248
00:07:48,849 --> 00:07:49,679
That sounds harmless

249
00:07:49,679 --> 00:07:51,479
But the Moon has been doing a much more

250
00:07:51,479 --> 00:07:52,479
important job:

251
00:07:52,479 --> 00:07:54,239
holding Earth steady

252
00:07:57,717 --> 00:07:59,787
Earth is tilted about twenty-three and a half

253
00:07:59,787 --> 00:08:00,267
degrees

254
00:08:00,267 --> 00:08:02,850
That tilt gives us our seasons

255
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:03,870
Thanks to the Moon,

256
00:08:03,870 --> 00:08:05,230
it only rocks gently,

257
00:08:05,230 --> 00:08:07,470
between about twenty-two and twenty-four and a

258
00:08:07,470 --> 00:08:08,865
half degrees

259
00:08:09,015 --> 00:08:09,725
Without the Moon,

260
00:08:09,725 --> 00:08:11,765
the pull of the Sun and the other planets

261
00:08:11,765 --> 00:08:14,365
could slowly push Earth's tilt much further

262
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:17,429
In 1993 a famous study suggested that a

263
00:08:17,429 --> 00:08:19,749
moonless Earth could tilt anywhere from almost

264
00:08:19,749 --> 00:08:21,829
zero to 85 degrees

265
00:08:22,286 --> 00:08:24,196
Newer simulations say the swings would be

266
00:08:24,196 --> 00:08:25,316
slower and smaller,

267
00:08:25,316 --> 00:08:27,916
but still large enough to rewrite our climate

268
00:08:28,393 --> 00:08:29,543
With almost no tilt,

269
00:08:29,543 --> 00:08:30,983
there would be no seasons at all

270
00:08:30,983 --> 00:08:32,023
With a huge tilt,

271
00:08:32,023 --> 00:08:33,983
one pole could face the Sun for months,

272
00:08:33,983 --> 00:08:36,382
while the other freezes in darkness

273
00:08:36,532 --> 00:08:38,242
Our neighbour Mars has no big moon

274
00:08:38,242 --> 00:08:39,722
Its tilt swings widely,

275
00:08:39,722 --> 00:08:42,082
and scientists think its ice has moved across

276
00:08:42,082 --> 00:08:44,045
the planet many times

277
00:08:44,195 --> 00:08:45,665
Which leaves one last question:

278
00:08:45,665 --> 00:08:48,265
what if the Moon had never existed at all?

279
00:08:51,983 --> 00:08:54,213
Most scientists think the Moon was born about

280
00:08:54,213 --> 00:08:56,133
four and a half billion years ago,

281
00:08:56,133 --> 00:08:58,213
when a planet the size of Mars smashed into

282
00:08:58,213 --> 00:08:59,543
the young Earth

283
00:08:59,693 --> 00:09:01,243
The debris formed a glowing ring,

284
00:09:01,243 --> 00:09:03,323
and the ring pulled itself together into the

285
00:09:03,323 --> 00:09:04,315
Moon

286
00:09:04,465 --> 00:09:04,895
Back then,

287
00:09:04,895 --> 00:09:06,335
the Moon was much closer,

288
00:09:06,335 --> 00:09:07,775
and Earth spun much faster

289
00:09:07,775 --> 00:09:10,095
A day may have lasted only about five or

290
00:09:10,095 --> 00:09:11,490
six hours

291
00:09:11,640 --> 00:09:12,710
Over billions of years,

292
00:09:12,710 --> 00:09:15,230
the Moon's brakes slowed us down to twenty-four

293
00:09:15,230 --> 00:09:16,297
hours

294
00:09:16,447 --> 00:09:17,197
Without the Moon,

295
00:09:17,197 --> 00:09:19,557
some estimates say a day today might last only

296
00:09:19,557 --> 00:09:21,313
six to twelve hours

297
00:09:21,463 --> 00:09:23,773
Some researchers even think strong tides helped

298
00:09:23,773 --> 00:09:25,533
mix chemicals in coastal pools,

299
00:09:25,533 --> 00:09:27,827
where early life may have begun

300
00:09:27,977 --> 00:09:28,887
So without the Moon,

301
00:09:28,887 --> 00:09:30,127
Earth would still be here

302
00:09:30,127 --> 00:09:31,767
But it would be a very different world,

303
00:09:31,767 --> 00:09:34,367
and we might not be here to see it

304
00:09:34,650 --> 00:09:36,240
The Moon is more than a light in the

305
00:09:36,240 --> 00:09:36,480
sky

306
00:09:36,480 --> 00:09:37,720
It moves our oceans,

307
00:09:37,720 --> 00:09:40,120
keeps our seasons steady and sets the rhythm of

308
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:40,805
life

309
00:09:40,955 --> 00:09:42,425
If the Moon disappeared tonight,

310
00:09:42,425 --> 00:09:43,225
we would survive

311
00:09:43,225 --> 00:09:45,825
But our planet would slowly become a stranger

312
00:09:46,042 --> 00:09:47,672
Which change would you miss the most:

313
00:09:47,672 --> 00:09:48,592
moonlit nights,

314
00:09:48,592 --> 00:09:49,352
the tides,

315
00:09:49,352 --> 00:09:50,232
or eclipses?

316
00:09:50,232 --> 00:09:51,849
Tell me in the comments

317
00:09:51,999 --> 00:09:54,599
Subscribe so you don't miss the next episode
